small-bodies-ask = How to measure a millet grain better than to a millimetre?
small-bodies-title = Measuring small bodies by the method of rows
small-bodies-question = The ruler’s divisions are 1 mm, and a millet grain is only about two divisions across, a pea six or seven. We measure one pea, then a row of peas, a row of millet, and wind a thread on a rod. How to measure a grain better than to a millimetre?
small-bodies-choices = Lay many grains in a row and divide the row’s length by their number
small-bodies-choices-2 = Measure one grain very carefully
small-bodies-choices-3 = No way: a ruler measures no better than its division
small-bodies-answer-d2 = {"."}
small-bodies-answer-why = A ruler gives a length only to a division: its error is half a division, 0.5 mm, whatever you measure. For a 6–7 mm pea that is several per cent already, and a millet grain or a thread cannot be measured by one ruler at all. But when we measure a row of N alike bodies, that half millimetre is for the whole row — just 0.5/N mm per body. So the longer the row, the better d = L/N. The differences between the peas average out the same way: we get the mean size. This is how the thickness of a sheet (a stack), of a wire (a winding) are measured — even molecules, magnified.
small-bodies-answer-say = A millet grain is { $L }: a row of { $n } grains gave hundredths of a millimetre.
small-bodies-text-parts = One pea — { $L } ± 0.5 mm: an error of about { $value } %
small-bodies-text-parts-2 = { $body } — { $L }, d = { $L } / { $n } ≈ { $L2 } (± { $value } mm)
small-bodies-answer-text = {" "}A row divides the ruler’s error by the number of bodies: the thread, thinner than a division, came out to hundredths of a millimetre.
